  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nHeading into Wednesday, the mid level closed low begins to open up \nand slides further north away from the region moving into \nWednesday night. At the same time, the strong mid level ridge in \nthe western Atlantic will push closer to the Southeast Coast and \nSouth Florida will remain on the southwestern periphery of this \nridge. At the surface, high pressure will continue to push \nfurther over the Florida Peninsula and into the eastern Gulf \nduring this time frame. This will allow for easterly winds to \nbecome established on Wednesday gradually increasing as the day \nprogresses. With plenty of moisture still remaining in place, \nconvective initiation will remain sea breeze driven. However, the\nhighest focus of showers and storms will shift to the interior \nand Southwest Florida due to the easterly wind flow setting up. \nChances for strong thunderstorm development will be rather \nlimited, however, it cannot be completely ruled out especially \nwhere mesoscale boundary collisions take place, High temperatures \non Wednesday will generally remain in the lower 90s and peak heat \nindex values will range between 103 and 108 degrees. \n\nHeading into Thursday and Friday, the latest global and ensemble \nsuite continue to show a TUTT feature passing just to the north \nof the Bahamas on Thursday and then approaching the Florida east \ncoast heading into Friday. The latest trends in the global and \nensemble guidance has started to come into slightly better \nagreement with keeping most of the energy associated with this \nfeature off to the north of the region over Central and Northern \nFlorida. Uncertainty still remains high though as any deviation \nfurther to the south would result in higher rainfall chances for \nSouth Florida especially heading into Friday. This will continue \nto be monitored as the week progresses as this will play a role \nin shower and thunderstorm chances towards the end of the week. In\nany event, easterly wind flow does remain in place and strengthens\nduring this time frame as the TUTT moves closer to the region on \nThursday and Friday. High temperatures will rise into the lower \n90s across most areas with peak heat index values ranging between \n103 and 108.\n\nDuring the upcoming weekend, mid level ridging will try to push back \nin over the region as surface high pressure in the western Atlantic \nremains in place. This will allow for shower and thunderstorm \nactivity to be sea breeze driven, with the highest chances of \nshowers and storms remaining over the interior and west coast due to \nlight to moderate east to southeasterly winds prevailing. High \ntemperatures look to remain slightly above climatological normals \nduring this time frame as mid level ridging becomes established over \nthe region.",
